# FABLE-L2-ARG-ADDRESS-DIRTY-20260921-67.  The author's sticky rule, and its negatives.
#
#   sticky  <=>  x is a parameter, `@: x` was EVALUATED before x's first EXECUTED binding line,
#               and that line then executes and binds x to the body's own field.
#   A binding line is an executed own declaration or an executed assignment-declaration of the
#   same name (docs/L2_spec); whichever runs first decides.  From it to the end of the activation
#   every checkpoint publishes the current local value and none of them clears the state.
#
# Every method names its field differently (na .. ne): an explicit WRITE `node\x: v` resolves x
# in the FIRST method that has a field of that name, not in the current one -- a separate
# translator defect, found while writing this fixture and reported with it.
#
# Each line printed is "<case> <local> <graph field>".
#
# HOW STICKINESS IS SEEN WITH LEGAL L2 ONLY.  `node\x: v` writes the graph cell and not the
# activation's value.  A later checkpoint of a STICKY field publishes the local over it; a
# checkpoint of an ordinary field, whose dirty an earlier checkpoint cleared, leaves it alone.
# That is also what tells sticky from "raise dirty again after the call returns": the A2 and A4
# checkpoints follow no address-taking call at all.
fn: poke5 (@: int p) int
    \p: 5
    return: 0
end: poke5
fn: poke9 (@: int p) int
    \p: 9
    return: 0
end: poke9
fn: nop () int
    return: 0
end: nop

# A. `@: na` BEFORE the first binding line, then the binding: sticky to the end.
#    One checkpoint before the address-taking call (nop) and five after the binding.
fn: before_bind (int: na) int
    nop()
    poke5(@: na)
    int: na
    na: na + 1
    nop()
    c.printf: "A1 %d %d\n" na node\na
    node\na: 100
    nop()
    c.printf: "A2 %d %d\n" na node\na
    poke9(@: na)
    c.printf: "A3 %d %d\n" na node\na
    node\na: 200
    nop()
    nop()
    c.printf: "A4 %d %d\n" na node\na
    return: 0
end: before_bind

# B. NEVER BOUND: the only binding line sits under a false condition and never executes, so the
#    parameter stayed a plain local -- the callee's write is seen, nothing is published, and the
#    checkpoints that follow have nothing to do.  Only the LOCAL can be printed: a field made by
#    assignment alone cannot be read through `node\x` by legal L2 today (a separate limit, reported
#    with this fixture), and a declared field would be bound by its declaration.  What this case
#    pins is that such an activation RUNS: a translator that made every address-taken parameter
#    sticky publishes here into a cell that was never loaded, and the program dies at `nop()`.
fn: never_bound (int: nb; int: go) int
    poke5(@: nb)
    if: go != 0
        nb: nb + 1
    nop()
    nop()
    c.printf: "B %d\n" nb
    return: 0
end: never_bound

# C. The binding FIRST, `@: nc` after it: ordinary dirty.  The checkpoint before the call
#    publishes and clears; the write through the address is no assignment and is not published.
fn: after_bind (int: nc) int
    int: nc
    nc: nc + 1
    nop()
    c.printf: "C1 %d %d\n" nc node\nc
    poke9(@: nc)
    c.printf: "C2 %d %d\n" nc node\nc
    node\nc: 100
    nop()
    c.printf: "C3 %d %d\n" nc node\nc
    return: 0
end: after_bind

# D. One method, both orders: the condition decides at RUN time whether the address comes before
#    the binding.  A translator that decides stickiness from the text alone fails one of the two.
fn: either (int: nd; int: early) int
    if: early != 0
        poke5(@: nd)
    int: nd
    nd: nd + 1
    if: early = 0
        poke5(@: nd)
    nop()
    node\nd: 100
    nop()
    c.printf: "D%d %d %d\n" early nd node\nd
    return: 0
end: either

# E. The DECLARATION is a binding line too: declared first, address after, assignment last --
#    the address came after the first executed binding line, so this is ordinary, not sticky.
fn: decl_binds (int: ne) int
    int: ne
    poke5(@: ne)
    ne: ne + 1
    nop()
    node\ne: 100
    nop()
    c.printf: "E %d %d\n" ne node\ne
    return: 0
end: decl_binds

fn: main () int
    before_bind(3)
    never_bound(3, 0)
    after_bind(3)
    either(3, 1)
    either(3, 0)
    decl_binds(3)
    return: 0
end: main
